.TH nm "" "Command"
.SH NAME
nm \- print name list
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBnm [\-adgnopru] [\fIfile ...\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
nm -- print name list
Usage:	nm [ options ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-a	Print all symbols
	-d	Print only definitions
	-g	Print only global symbols
	-n	Sort numerically (default: sort by name)
	-o	Prepend file or member name to each line
	-p	Print in symbol table order (no sort)
	-r	Reverse order of sort
	-u	Print undefined symbols
'file' may be an object file or an archive.
.HE
.B nm
prints the symbol table of each
.IR file .
Each
.I file
may be a linked
.B l.out
object or executable, or an archive (a file beginning with the
archive magic number) made by
.BR ar ;
for an archive,
.B nm
prints the symbols of every member except
.BR __.SYMDEF .
With no
.I file
argument,
.B nm
reads
.BR l.out .
.PP
By default,
.B nm
prints only symbols whose names look like C identifiers (a trailing
underscore is stripped before printing, or the name is at least
seven characters long); the
.B \-a
option disables this filtering.
Symbols are sorted by name unless
.B \-n
or
.B \-p
is given.
Each line gives the symbol's segment (one of
.BR si ", " pi ", " bi ", " sd ", " pd ", " bd ", or " d
for defined symbols, blank for a common symbol, or
.B u
or
.B U
for an undefined reference, capitalized if the symbol is global) and
either its address or, for a common symbol, its size, followed by the
symbol name.
.PP
When more than one
.I file
is given, or a
.I file
is an archive,
.B nm
prints a heading line naming the file or archive member before its
symbols, unless
.B \-o
was given, in which case the file or member name is instead prepended
to every symbol line.
.IP \fB\-a\fR
Print all symbols, including ones that are not C identifiers.
.IP \fB\-d\fR
Print only defined symbols (suppress undefined references).
.IP \fB\-g\fR
Print only global (external) symbols.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Sort symbols numerically by address, instead of by name.
.IP \fB\-o\fR
Prepend the file (or, for an archive member, the member) name to each
output line instead of printing it as a heading.
.IP \fB\-p\fR
Print symbols in the order they occur in the symbol table; do not
sort them.
.IP \fB\-r\fR
Reverse the sort order.
.IP \fB\-u\fR
Print only undefined symbols.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ar ,
.BR ld ,
.BR size ,
.B strip
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B nm
complains if a
.I file
cannot be opened, is not a recognized object file, or has no symbol
table.
